(BUGS_FOUND #84: `isn't` is a documented spelling of `not` - LANGUAGE.md's
 Logical Operators table - that the lexer could never build, because
 `read_word` stopped dead at the apostrophe. `isn't` means `is not`, so
 each pair below prints the same line twice: the contraction first, then
 the spelled-out form it stands for.)

a number called v1 is 20.
a number called v2 is 6.

(Not equal)
If v1 isn't v2 then, print "differ". Otherwise, print "same".
If v1 is not v2 then, print "differ". Otherwise, print "same".

(Not equal, written out)
If v1 isn't equal to v2 then, print "differ". Otherwise, print "same".
If v1 is not equal to v2 then, print "differ". Otherwise, print "same".

(A property, where the answer is no - the negation has to be able to fail)
If v1 isn't even then, print "odd". Otherwise, print "even".
If v1 is not even then, print "odd". Otherwise, print "even".

(A literal on the right, where the two are equal)
If v1 isn't 20 then, print "not twenty". Otherwise, print "twenty".
If v1 is not 20 then, print "not twenty". Otherwise, print "twenty".

(Case is not significant, as for every other keyword)
If v1 ISN'T v2 then, print "differ". Otherwise, print "same".
